WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:08.400
LARRY PAUL ARNN: It's
my honor to introduce

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.080
to you a great man.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.120
That's a habit, because I got
to also introduce Natasha--

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.050
not a great man, a great woman.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:24.000
Richard Corcoran is the
Florida Education Commissioner

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.140
and has purview
over K through 12.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.910
He's one of the best in the
country at his job, which

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.327
is not quite damning
with faint praise,

00:00:35.327 --> 00:00:37.410
because there's two or
three that are pretty good.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.380
His wife is a classical
education woman herself

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:44.235
and a mother of six.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.750
His father was a soldier, and
he was in the Army Reserve.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.150
The cool thing about
him, in my opinion,

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:02.990
is that I learned
about six years ago--

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:06.650
because we got into education
reform and founding charter

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:07.730
schools.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:11.900
And the best place in the
country to do it is Florida.

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.130
And I started learning
how that came to be.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.850
First I met a man named
Michael Bileca, who was

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.250
Speaker of the Florida House.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:24.410
And then I met our speaker
today, who Michael Bileca--

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.720
did you succeed Michael
or did he succeed you?

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:32.190
I see.

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:35.730
They were in the Florida
House at the same time.

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:38.520
And then, of course,
the great Ron DeSantis--

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.790
and they have been step-by-step
reforming the charter school

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:45.710
laws and in Florida
so that you can do it.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.210
And there are a lot of detailed
things that have to be done,

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:51.540
like charter schools
are public schools.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.280
They have a measure
of independence

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.890
from the government, and
how wide that measure is

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.690
the decisive factor.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.240
But also, when does the
education money come

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.980
to the school, and
in what quantity

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.000
so you can actually do it?

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.870
And they just have
been working on that

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:11.370
and figuring it out and
coming to see that for years.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.867
And they have made Florida a
paradise for charter education.

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:16.200
And they're still working on it.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.700
I was impressed more
than anything else

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.770
about the acts of
friendship that

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.250
have gone on that have kept
continuity in that effort,

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:31.040
because that's very
difficult stuff to do--

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.690
to stand up against the constant
bureaucratization of everything

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:38.330
in the country, to
try to liberate people

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:42.530
to do wonderful jobs like
running schools and raising

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.630
their own children--

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.050
that is, in the short term,
often a thankless task.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:50.450
And it takes a lot of
skill and commitment,

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:53.300
and it's rare to see people
who sustain that together

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.630
for so long.

00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:58.090
And that's what's
happened in Florida.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:00.850
And we're going to hear about
whatever Richard Corcoran wants

00:03:00.850 --> 00:03:02.650
to say, but I will
say that he is

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:05.830
one of the most important men
in the United States today,

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:07.660
and we are honored
to have him here.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:08.500
Richard Corcoran.

00:03:16.525 --> 00:03:18.900
RICHARD CORCORAN: First, I
want to say it's a great honor

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:20.025
to be at Hillsdale College.

00:03:20.025 --> 00:03:23.880
Obviously, being a
conservative all my life,

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.160
Hillsdale has played
an active role

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.610
in whether it's just the
monthly Imprimis or people I've

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.950
known who come to Hillsdale,
watching the speakers--

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.050
it's a fantastic college.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:36.210
And you guys, we've been
here two days-- just

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.970
fantastic meetings with
the faculty, with Dr. Arnn.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:40.890
So it's a pleasure to be here.

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:43.960
I'll give you one warning,
though, before I start.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:46.057
And when you're
Speaker of the House

00:03:46.057 --> 00:03:48.390
and then become Education
Commissioner, you get invited.

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:50.760
And so I was invited
to go to a nursing home

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.030
and give a speech on
American exceptionalism.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:55.240
And so I thought,
OK, I'll do that.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:58.020
And they had all the residents
gathered, and I give a speech

00:03:58.020 --> 00:03:59.970
and talked about
American exceptionalism.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:01.470
And then the director
said there was

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.270
one gentleman who was too sick.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:05.370
He couldn't make
it out of his room,

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.440
and he asked if you'd be kind
enough to give him a speech.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:09.360
And so I thought, OK.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.970
So I went into his
room, and I gave--

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:14.430
of course I shortened it,
and I gave him the speech.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:16.470
And I'm trying to say
all these important lines

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.220
about American
exceptionalism, and he just

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.910
won't even look at me,
won't budge, won't move.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.130
I say another one.

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:22.630
Nothing.

00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:24.000
I get done with the speech.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.290
Nothing.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.040
And so finally as I'm
walking to the door,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.315
I think I've got to say
something to this gentleman.

00:04:29.315 --> 00:04:30.690
And so I turn to
him, and I said,

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:33.780
sir, I really, really
hope you get better.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.220
And he kind of
pops up in his bed,

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:36.553
and he goes, I hope you do, too.

00:04:36.553 --> 00:04:41.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:44.560
So I always like to warn
an audience before I speak,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.490
because now you've
been fairly warned.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:51.160
I do want to talk,
though, a little bit,

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:57.880
which at this college, you guys
could lecture ad nauseum me

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.138
on the topic.

00:04:59.138 --> 00:05:00.430
But I grew up with two parents.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:01.720
My parents were older.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:03.490
Both have passed.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.518
Both had very difficult lives.

00:05:05.518 --> 00:05:07.060
My dad was orphaned
when he was two--

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:13.990
grew up in orphanages and foster
homes, went into World War II,

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:19.750
and then after World War II
got into the Foreign Service.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:23.950
My mother grew up in England
during the Great Depression.

00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:26.770
Their family went
through the entire crash.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:28.390
They had no money,
and so her father

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:31.648
took a job managing a
tea plantation in India,

00:05:31.648 --> 00:05:33.940
and that's where she was
actually born-- in Darjeeling.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:35.982
And so but they grew up,
and so they would always

00:05:35.982 --> 00:05:39.670
say to us in these two
pivotal moments in time--

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:41.800
the Great Depression
and World War II.

00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:44.890
And so all we would ever hear
was, philosophy was everything.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:46.210
You ought to have a philosophy.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:47.388
What is your philosophy?

00:05:47.388 --> 00:05:48.430
And it could be anything.

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:49.840
If you're thinking
about having children,

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.458
what's your philosophy on how--
what do they look like at 18?

00:05:52.458 --> 00:05:53.750
What's your philosophy on work?

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:55.930
What's your philosophy
in all aspects of life?

00:05:55.930 --> 00:05:58.780
That philosophy was
everything, and it

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:03.070
was because their lives were
completely altered, changed,

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:05.650
and controlled by the
philosophies of others,

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:07.850
both economically
and physically.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:10.270
And so they would say,
either you have a philosophy

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.130
or you don't, but if
you don't, that's OK,

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:14.290
but someone else has one,
and you will absolutely

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:15.820
be moved by their philosophy.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:20.590
And so all of that just
spawned that rich thought

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:23.320
throughout-- we would literally
have conversations and say,

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:24.370
is man good or bad?

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:26.180
We would have to
answer that question.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.152
And then when I became
a Speaker later in life,

00:06:28.152 --> 00:06:29.860
these philosophies
start coming together.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:31.510
And you realize that--

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:33.165
I will tell you,
the best legislators

00:06:33.165 --> 00:06:34.540
that I would serve
with, the ones

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.700
who really understood
the role of government

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.880
and what it means to be a
successful self-governing

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.710
society, would be
ones that would

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:45.855
say that man was inherently bad
as opposed to inherently good.

00:06:45.855 --> 00:06:47.230
The ones who were
inherently good

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.060
were the ones who would
constantly come up

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:50.560
with a great society
and everything

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:54.620
along those lines on a
microcosm at a state level.

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.380
So getting the
position of education,

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:59.140
the first thing I
thought of was what

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:00.550
is the philosophy of education?

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:02.500
And the sad truth--

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.070
many of them conservatives--

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:06.070
how many times will we
hear we need more welders

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:07.695
than philosophers?

00:07:07.695 --> 00:07:09.070
What is the purpose
of education?

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:10.270
And the modern day--

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:13.600
even among Republican
rank and file--

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:17.650
version of education is it's
a means to a job, a career,

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:19.300
or something along those lines.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:20.440
And it's so much more.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:23.170
And I didn't even know
this until I spent time

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:24.880
with Dr. Arnn today.

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.730
But obviously, one
of my favorite folks

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:29.980
that I followed throughout
time is Frederick Douglass,

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:32.105
and I did not know he had
been on this campus twice

00:07:32.105 --> 00:07:37.218
and have tremendous
statues and quotes

00:07:37.218 --> 00:07:39.010
and what have you about
Frederick Douglass.

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:40.510
But he has the greatest
quote of education.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:42.220
When I took over as
Education Commissioner,

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:43.887
the first thing I did
was tear down most

00:07:43.887 --> 00:07:45.730
of the stuff in the building.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.980
And I put up huge
quotes, one by Washington

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:49.600
and one by Frederick Douglass.

00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:51.220
Because I thought
I wanted everyone

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:52.970
who walked into the
building to understand

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:55.210
what the philosophy and the
purpose of education was.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:58.180
But he has this just fantastic
quote I'll paraphrase,

00:07:58.180 --> 00:08:00.310
where he says,
education is freedom.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:03.272
This is a guy who escaped
slavery, came to the north.

00:08:03.272 --> 00:08:04.480
He goes education is freedom.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:05.710
It's emancipation.

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:07.990
It is the uplifting
of the human soul

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:10.090
to the glorious light of truth.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:14.650
And to deny any individual
a world-class education

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.600
is the greatest crime
against humanity.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:19.535
And so when you think about
what education is-- and again,

00:08:19.535 --> 00:08:21.160
nobody knows it better
than Hillsdale--

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:24.040
but it is about
creating great citizens.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.020
You look at the
Fortune 500 CEOs,

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:28.150
and they all say, we want
people who can critically

00:08:28.150 --> 00:08:30.580
think, who can problem
solve, who can communicate.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.413
We really don't care
what their degree's in.

00:08:33.413 --> 00:08:35.830
I always quote Steve Jobs,
because people look at me like,

00:08:35.830 --> 00:08:38.890
oh, you're just a
liberal arts guy.

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:43.419
You don't really understand
the role of STEM in society.

00:08:43.419 --> 00:08:45.985
And I say, OK, well, let's
take the first trillion dollar

00:08:45.985 --> 00:08:47.980
company in America-- is Apple.

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:51.070
The CEO of Apple-- also educated
in a liberal arts school

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:52.390
for the time he was there--

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:54.700
where he found
Helvetica text, and that

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:59.140
became the formation of
the Apple computer text.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:00.640
They said to him,
how did you become

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:02.320
the first trillion
dollar company?

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.055
And he would say, it
was easy because all

00:09:04.055 --> 00:09:04.930
the other companies--

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:06.352
Microsoft, Oracle,
name them all--

00:09:06.352 --> 00:09:07.810
they're computer
science companies.

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:08.812
And this is on YouTube.

00:09:08.812 --> 00:09:10.270
They're computer
science companies.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:13.540
He says, Apple-- we exist at
the intersection of computer

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:14.710
science and liberal arts.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:17.653
This I can teach you, and I
probably would rather do it.

00:09:17.653 --> 00:09:19.570
I'd rather not have to
detox you from whatever

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:21.528
you learned in college
or wherever you're from.

00:09:21.528 --> 00:09:22.480
This, I can't teach.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:23.863
Either you have it or you don't.

00:09:23.863 --> 00:09:25.780
And that's why everything--
you look at Apple.

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:27.310
Everything they did--
every advertisement--

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:29.393
it was never about selling
computers, to the point

00:09:29.393 --> 00:09:31.900
now that you have guys who have
become millionaires writing

00:09:31.900 --> 00:09:34.943
books about Steve Jobs' why.

00:09:34.943 --> 00:09:36.610
But he's literally
just saying that this

00:09:36.610 --> 00:09:39.390
is the why that Steve Jobs
had about the liberal arts

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.150
and computer science
intersection.

00:09:42.150 --> 00:09:46.620
But what it did was he
would sell think different.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:48.330
It's going to change your life--

00:09:48.330 --> 00:09:51.337
his famous commercial
about we're the crazy ones.

00:09:51.337 --> 00:09:52.170
You can't ignore us.

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:54.510
You can't-- narrated
by Richard Dreyfuss.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:56.955
It was this whole
concept of, as Dr. Arnn

00:09:56.955 --> 00:09:58.830
was saying earlier--
it's the true, the good,

00:09:58.830 --> 00:09:59.580
and the beautiful.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.160
And that's what built him
to this beautiful trillion

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:03.330
dollar company.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:06.000
And so all of that
gets to the two

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.330
points I do want to
impress upon you as I

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:10.830
got to the department-- is that
now you know the philosophy.

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:12.810
You know the one
thing that we can

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:16.260
do to change people's lives.

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.180
We talked about it last night.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:21.030
We can't force everyone to be
raised in a two-parent home,

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:24.150
but we know the consequences
or the derivative

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:25.770
of that is just enormous.

00:10:25.770 --> 00:10:28.620
But what we can do,
and what we are doing,

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:31.140
is trying to give every child
that world-class education--

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:34.290
help them become good citizens,
good spouses, good community

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:38.670
leaders, and recognize the
inherent value in education.

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:39.870
But we're not there.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.400
And not only are
we not there, we

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:44.320
struggle more and more
and more to get there.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:47.100
And so I'll give you
the facts in Florida.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:50.730
And on the National Assessment
of Educational Progress, which

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.935
all the states take,
we're in the top 4

00:10:52.935 --> 00:10:55.590
for reading in fourth grade.

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:58.350
The number one is Massachusetts.

00:10:58.350 --> 00:10:59.280
Pretty perennially.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.658
I think it's going to
change as a result of COVID,

00:11:01.658 --> 00:11:03.450
because we're the only
state that opened up

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.742
our schools-- face-to-face
instruction to any child who

00:11:05.742 --> 00:11:06.510
wanted it.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.040
Boston is still closed.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:10.440
And so, but Massachusetts
is number one.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:13.500
They're number one, and they're
like 45% on the NAEP exam

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.610
at or above basic.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:18.267
Which means I always say 55%--

00:11:18.267 --> 00:11:20.100
the number one state
and the richest country

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:21.270
in the history of mankind--

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:24.600
the number one state
has 55%, give or take--

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:27.690
Florida, we're like at
39, so we're over 60%--

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:32.400
of our fourth graders that
are somewhat, partially,

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:34.640
or fully illiterate.

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.430
And so now take Florida.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:39.100
We have our own state assessment
we give in third grade.

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:40.960
In our third grade
assessment, we basically

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:42.880
have similar numbers--

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:47.620
42%, 44% of our third
graders in English

00:11:47.620 --> 00:11:51.340
are either partially
fully illiterate.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:53.310
They're either
getting 1s and 2s.

00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:58.210
And when you have
that, what you're doing

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:00.880
in a society is not only
are you creating people

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:04.360
who have no concept of what
it means to self govern or be

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:05.920
a citizen--

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.560
they have no hope.

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:13.330
And the most dangerous person
in any realm of society--

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:16.000
in life, in a school
yard, wherever, it

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:18.602
doesn't matter-- take hope
away from a human being,

00:12:18.602 --> 00:12:20.560
and they become extremely,
extremely dangerous.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:22.630
And I think you see
that play itself out.

00:12:22.630 --> 00:12:24.550
And so how do you move that?

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:26.650
I say to my staff
all the time, we

00:12:26.650 --> 00:12:29.590
ought to just send letters
to every single third grade

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:33.190
parent, which is
literally 55% of them,

00:12:33.190 --> 00:12:36.910
and say, hey, dear Mrs. Jones.

00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:38.050
Billy's a great kid.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.770
Enjoyed having him in class.

00:12:39.770 --> 00:12:41.740
But here's the bottom line--

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.270
there's no hope for him.

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:44.630
He's toast.

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:46.120
There's nothing we can do.

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.470
Because we know this fact, too.

00:12:47.470 --> 00:12:49.660
If you're a 1 and
2 in third grade--

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:51.880
because we had 20 years of
accountability history--

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:54.970
then I asked my staff, give me
every single graduate of third

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:56.470
grade that became
a tenth grader--

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:58.178
because now we have
20 years of history--

00:12:58.178 --> 00:13:00.310
and tell me what they
were in 10th grade

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:03.220
the last time we
give the ESA exam.

00:13:03.220 --> 00:13:04.060
And guess what?

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:07.330
86% who are 1s and
2s-- either partially

00:13:07.330 --> 00:13:09.790
or functionally illiterate--
are partially or functionally

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:11.620
illiterate in 10th grade.

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:13.300
And the only thing
that changes them--

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:16.000
obviously Hillsdale is
a significant factor--

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:17.597
if they move to some other--

00:13:17.597 --> 00:13:19.680
the three things that
changed education in America

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.055
are accountability, number
one-- more than anything else.

00:13:22.055 --> 00:13:24.490
Choice number two-- but if
they somehow find their way

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:26.890
into a Choice school-- an
excellent Choice school--

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:28.940
if they somehow find
an excellent teacher--

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:32.530
maybe they get a seventh
grade middle school Hillsdale

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:33.515
graduate who says--

00:13:33.515 --> 00:13:35.140
and what they're
going to do is they're

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:37.827
going to go back and start
like at kindergarten, first,

00:13:37.827 --> 00:13:39.910
and second grade and get
those children caught up.

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:45.920
But otherwise, 86% are still
functionally illiterate.

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:46.900
The converse is true.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:50.020
If you're a 3, 4,
or 5, it's about 85%

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:51.190
are still 3s, 4s, and 5s.

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:55.060
So whatever happens between
pre-K or birth and third

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:55.660
grade--

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:58.360
once we lose them there,
we lose them for life.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.880
And they become, what I
would say is not all of them,

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:05.140
but a lot of them become
very, very dangerous.

00:14:05.140 --> 00:14:06.350
And that's a problem.

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:08.440
And so how do you fix it?

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:11.530
What we have done in Florida,
and moving to do in Florida, is

00:14:11.530 --> 00:14:12.370
we're going all in.

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:14.620
The stats are just-- as you
dig into it more and more,

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:15.880
the stats are staggering.

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:17.950
61% of low income
kids don't have

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:19.750
a single book in their home.

00:14:19.750 --> 00:14:22.318
And if they don't have a
single book in their home,

00:14:22.318 --> 00:14:23.360
no one's reading to them.

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:24.760
If you're a parent,
most of you guys

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.950
have probably read to your
children at least 20 minutes

00:14:26.950 --> 00:14:28.750
a day, but that's the
minimal goal we try to say--

00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:29.740
20 minutes a day.

00:14:29.740 --> 00:14:32.020
If you read 20 minutes
a day to your child,

00:14:32.020 --> 00:14:34.600
they finish in the top
10%, statistically,

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:35.980
on standardized tests.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:38.890
If you read 1 minute a day to
your child or no minutes a day

00:14:38.890 --> 00:14:41.470
to your child,
they basically are

00:14:41.470 --> 00:14:45.040
the corpus of your
dropouts, your failures,

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:47.480
and/or your
illiterate population.

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:51.610
And so if we don't go all in and
wrestle with those kids in that

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:54.280
pre-K to third grade realm-- we
were talking the other night,

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:56.627
and I said, if I was to do
something else much like

00:14:56.627 --> 00:14:57.460
Hillsdale is doing--

00:14:57.460 --> 00:15:00.610
I'd love to see the
same Hillsdale model,

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:05.440
but to rapidly expand and
go into pre-K to third grade

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.000
and going into
every single state

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:08.920
as fast as we can in
a big way, because we

00:15:08.920 --> 00:15:11.140
know that once we lose
them in third grade,

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:12.790
we lose them for life.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:15.045
So that's-- we're
doing everything.

00:15:15.045 --> 00:15:16.420
We're trying to
do at Peace Corps

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:18.610
and have great graduates
like Hillsdale go in

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.550
and read to these kids
from pre-K to third grade.

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:24.048
We're having literally
a book a month club.

00:15:24.048 --> 00:15:25.090
These kids are very poor.

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:25.965
They've never had it.

00:15:25.965 --> 00:15:28.405
I know for me, when I get
the Amazon truck pull in,

00:15:28.405 --> 00:15:30.280
and I know it's a new
golf club or something,

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:31.720
I'm pretty excited.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:36.100
So imagine a child who doesn't
even celebrate Christmas now

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:38.687
having a book delivered to
his house on a daily basis.

00:15:38.687 --> 00:15:40.270
And that book is
tied to our standards

00:15:40.270 --> 00:15:43.660
that we completely rewrote,
and it's content rich.

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:46.930
And so not only are they
getting that great literature,

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:48.670
they're learning the
same book in school

00:15:48.670 --> 00:15:50.920
at the same time
with their mentors--

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:52.900
all at the same time.

00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:55.240
And hopefully it will
have that effect.

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:58.930
We've also basically gone all
in on the science of reading.

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:04.390
Modern day-- we all recognize
that the social experiment

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:07.375
of whole language was
an abject failure.

00:16:07.375 --> 00:16:09.250
But even now we don't
call it whole language.

00:16:09.250 --> 00:16:11.740
Now we have great terms--
blended literacy, whatever.

00:16:11.740 --> 00:16:14.110
But it's up and down in our
schools all across America--

00:16:14.110 --> 00:16:16.690
not obviously in
Hillsdale charter schools

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:18.560
or any classical
school for that matter.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:19.987
But if we don't
have that phonics,

00:16:19.987 --> 00:16:22.570
and the phonemic awareness, and
the literacy, and the fluency,

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:25.237
and the vocabulary, you're never
going to get the comprehension.

00:16:25.237 --> 00:16:28.120
And so we're going all in and
fighting with our publishers

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:31.780
that we will not have a single
textbook in a classroom that

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:33.487
doesn't have the
science of reading.

00:16:33.487 --> 00:16:35.070
Hopefully, all of
that stuff and we'll

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:38.160
start seeing
quickly improvements

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.230
in our students' education.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:44.400
So I know that it sounds--

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:46.320
as an aside to
that, I'll tell you.

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:47.820
Having been in the
legislature, it's

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:49.470
an amazing thing in education.

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:52.440
And you guys have seen it
as you go into other states.

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:54.708
But when I was in the
legislature, if somebody

00:16:54.708 --> 00:16:57.000
came before-- and I was on
an environmental committee--

00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:59.010
and they said, hey, we
have this magic potion.

00:16:59.010 --> 00:17:00.480
We can drop it in
Lake Okeechobee,

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:02.550
and immediately there'll
be no blue-green algae.

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:04.050
There would be absolute outcry.

00:17:04.050 --> 00:17:05.724
People would be upset.

00:17:05.724 --> 00:17:07.599
They'd almost be thrown
out of the committee.

00:17:07.599 --> 00:17:08.680
This is ridiculous.

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:11.010
We would have to study that
for six or seven years.

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:14.460
We'd want to make sure
everything was done correctly

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:16.500
and all our scientific
data was correct.

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:18.750
This will never happen in
the next six or seven years.

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:20.640
Now conversely, go to
an education committee,

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:23.550
and somebody comes in and says,
we have this magic potion.

00:17:23.550 --> 00:17:25.314
If we put it in
schools tomorrow,

00:17:25.314 --> 00:17:27.064
our kids will just be
phenomenal students.

00:17:27.064 --> 00:17:28.319
There like, let's try it.

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:31.500
I mean, there's no screening
or testing or anything,

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:35.685
to the point that when you look
at America on the national--

00:17:35.685 --> 00:17:37.560
where we compete with
the rest of the world--

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:41.500
America, we're like 24th,
28th, and like 35th and math,

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:43.080
science, and in English--

00:17:43.080 --> 00:17:44.430
you know, just abysmal.

00:17:44.430 --> 00:17:46.763
But when they did the study
and they looked at what does

00:17:46.763 --> 00:17:49.440
America do great, the
just wonderful irony--

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:50.797
which we still do today--

00:17:50.797 --> 00:17:52.380
the two things that
America-- everyone

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:54.930
says nobody can compete
with-- number one

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:56.890
is we have the
prettiest buildings.

00:17:56.890 --> 00:18:00.120
And so to quote, you can
teach Plato under a tree.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:02.620
We don't even teach Plato, but
we have a beautiful building.

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:04.328
So don't worry about
going under the tree

00:18:04.328 --> 00:18:05.520
and learning something.

00:18:05.520 --> 00:18:07.140
And then the second
part is that we

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:10.080
have absolutely without
question more tech in classrooms

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:10.980
than anybody else.

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:14.770
And it's been, quite frankly,
overwhelmingly a failure,

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:15.700
not a success.

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:17.617
And so those are the two
things we score well,

00:18:17.617 --> 00:18:19.980
even though we're
24th, 28th, and 29th.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:24.090
All of that to say,
my dad and mom--

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:26.530
I'll start with them.

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:27.773
I do think there's optimism.

00:18:27.773 --> 00:18:29.440
I was talking a lot
to Dr. Arnn about it

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:31.060
with the universities
and colleges,

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:32.657
and we have a whole
group of folks

00:18:32.657 --> 00:18:34.990
that are boards of directors
from Flagler College trying

00:18:34.990 --> 00:18:37.220
to-- how do you replicate
what Hillsdale has done?

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:39.400
Is it possible and
can it be sustainable

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:41.440
and all of those questions?

00:18:41.440 --> 00:18:44.740
And Dr. Arnn basically
said, quoting Churchill,

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:46.840
but there is hope.

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:48.920
They're formed on
a broken truth,

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:51.170
and so there is hope
for us to crash that.

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:53.420
And that's something my
father and mother would tell--

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:54.837
I don't know if
you've heard this,

00:18:54.837 --> 00:18:57.400
but my dad would always say,
there's two little boys.

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:00.550
One's an eternal optimist, and
one's an eternal pessimist.

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:02.980
And so they go and they
get studied by doctors,

00:19:02.980 --> 00:19:06.040
and they say let's put
the optimist boy in a room

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:07.207
with a pile of manure.

00:19:07.207 --> 00:19:09.040
And let's take the
pessimist boy and put him

00:19:09.040 --> 00:19:11.980
in a room with toys, candies,
and everything under the sun.

00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:14.110
And they put the
boys in the room.

00:19:14.110 --> 00:19:16.590
They check on them,
and they see the boy--

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:17.950
the pessimist-- first.

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:19.660
And they go in,
and he's cowering

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:20.680
in the corner of a room.

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:22.990
Like little boy, why aren't
you playing with the candy?

00:19:22.990 --> 00:19:24.753
And he's like, I might get sick.

00:19:24.753 --> 00:19:26.170
Why don't you play
with the toys--

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:27.280
I don't know, the buttons?

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:28.497
I could swallow a button.

00:19:28.497 --> 00:19:29.080
It's like, OK.

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:29.920
That's a lost cause.

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:31.943
Let's go check on
the optimistic boy.

00:19:31.943 --> 00:19:33.610
And they go check on
the optimistic boy,

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:35.710
and they look in the window,
and he's jumping up and down.

00:19:35.710 --> 00:19:37.190
He's throwing the
manure everywhere.

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:38.023
It's on the ceiling.

00:19:38.023 --> 00:19:39.050
It's all over his body.

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:41.050
They go rushing into the
room, and they're like,

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:42.118
whoa, whoa, whoa.

00:19:42.118 --> 00:19:42.910
What are you doing?

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:43.930
Don't you it's a pile of manure?

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:45.190
And he goes, yeah, but
with all this manure,

00:19:45.190 --> 00:19:46.648
there's got to be
a pony somewhere.

00:19:46.648 --> 00:19:49.980
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:54.460
And so with that
analogy or anecdote

00:19:54.460 --> 00:19:57.940
and with ending with
Frederick Douglass in that it

00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:00.460
is far easier to raise
up healthy children

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:02.860
than it is to fix broken men--
another Frederick Douglass

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:03.940
quote.

00:20:03.940 --> 00:20:07.492
But the pony in
the story is what

00:20:07.492 --> 00:20:08.950
you guys are doing--
what Hillsdale

00:20:08.950 --> 00:20:10.870
is doing, what all
of you are doing--

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:14.020
educating the educators who
will be in those schools.

00:20:14.020 --> 00:20:16.300
It will hopefully
change our country

00:20:16.300 --> 00:20:18.130
and sustain us for
another 230 years--

00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:19.300
at least that's the hope.

00:20:19.300 --> 00:20:20.350
So thank you very much.

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:32.800
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:34.570
SPEAKER: Thank you,
Commissioner Corcoran.

00:20:34.570 --> 00:20:37.550
We now have time for some
Q&amp;A. If you have a question,

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:39.665
please raise your hand
and wait for a microphone

00:20:39.665 --> 00:20:40.540
to be brought to you.

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:49.240
AUDIENCE: Terrific talk.

00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:54.040
So I'm on the board of Northwest
Ohio Classical Academy--

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:57.460
BCS High School,
opened two years ago.

00:20:57.460 --> 00:21:00.250
Our outcomes are good,
and that is certainly

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:04.360
one of the motivations to
do the very heavy lift.

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:08.740
But the second motivation
is to encourage

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:13.330
others who may not necessarily
want a particularly

00:21:13.330 --> 00:21:16.810
classical education from
the Barney Charter School.

00:21:16.810 --> 00:21:20.500
To see them make good choices--

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:23.620
is there any reason to believe
that that won't happen?

00:21:27.940 --> 00:21:29.080
RICHARD CORCORAN: That--

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:32.050
SPEAKER: Even if they're not
at Barney Charter School,

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:33.310
we've got 24.

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:39.900
We've got 48 million
kids, K through 12.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.130
We need to change.

00:21:42.130 --> 00:21:49.450
There needs to be a vast
change in the education system

00:21:49.450 --> 00:21:51.150
so our kids will prosper.

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:52.260
That's great.

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:53.580
That's the first motivation.

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:56.100
But the second
motivation is we're

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:59.340
hoping that others will make
some good choices where they're

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:00.330
not making it.

00:22:00.330 --> 00:22:01.980
Are we having that effect?

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:03.780
Not necessarily in
Barney Charter School,

00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:05.010
but just in Florida--

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:08.550
are you making--
do you see schools

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:10.770
changing for the better?

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:14.580
RICHARD CORCORAN: Absolutely,
but it's volatile.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:18.780
So we just won a gubernatorial
race by 30,000 votes in a state

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:21.480
with 21 plus million people.

00:22:21.480 --> 00:22:24.420
And flip that 30,000 votes--

00:22:24.420 --> 00:22:28.320
I mean, if you followed Ron
DeSantis' opponent, Andrew

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:30.300
Gillum and what happened
to him afterwards.

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:31.680
I mean, we were that close.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:34.950
And if Andrew Gillum
became the governor,

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:38.130
along comes Gwen Graham
and all of the radical left

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:40.620
and what they do to
our school system.

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:42.810
What we've had in
Florida that's worked,

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:47.010
and we've had numerous, whether
you go back to the DeSantis--

00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:50.490
less than 1%, Rick Scott's
re-elect, less than 1%,

00:22:50.490 --> 00:22:52.890
first election, less than 1%.

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:55.710
For whatever reason, we barely
win these governor's races,

00:22:55.710 --> 00:22:58.000
but have won them since 1998.

00:22:58.000 --> 00:23:00.720
So we've had a long run
of Republican governors

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:02.610
and a long run of reform.

00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:04.615
So yes, when Governor
DeSantis gets elected,

00:23:04.615 --> 00:23:05.990
he can immediately
say, OK, we're

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:07.110
going to eliminate common core.

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:08.652
We're going to
rewrite the standards.

00:23:08.652 --> 00:23:10.470
We're going to make
them content rich.

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:12.720
We're going to have the
science of reading embedded

00:23:12.720 --> 00:23:14.230
into in into our standards.

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:16.230
We're going to increase
accountability, increase

00:23:16.230 --> 00:23:17.430
choice.

00:23:17.430 --> 00:23:19.927
We just got done Friday
from the legislative session

00:23:19.927 --> 00:23:20.760
and passed another--

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:24.990
1.2 million kids are eligible
for Choice in Florida.

00:23:24.990 --> 00:23:29.790
So it happens, but I will tell
you leadership is everything.

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:32.400
And as soon as that
leadership changes,

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:35.100
everything else does
too, unfortunately.

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:36.210
But that's the reality.

00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:40.020
McChrystal would say, I
have all these colonels.

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:41.070
I have 20 of them.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:44.850
I could take the best
colonel with the best

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:47.780
troop and the worst colonel
with the worst troop

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:48.780
and I could switch them.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:50.155
And immediately,
the best colonel

00:23:50.155 --> 00:23:52.500
is going to have the best
troop and the worst colonel's

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:53.750
going to have the worst troop.

00:23:53.750 --> 00:23:56.768
And it's the same-- and
that's not as ideological.

00:23:56.768 --> 00:23:58.560
But in the world that
we live in education,

00:23:58.560 --> 00:23:59.850
it's 100% ideological.

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:01.770
So in no time--

00:24:01.770 --> 00:24:05.463
what we have to do is cross the
Rubicon, and we're very close.

00:24:05.463 --> 00:24:07.380
I would say the three
things that have changed

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:09.690
education are accountability--

00:24:09.690 --> 00:24:10.530
has absolutely.

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:12.090
The second we
flipped on the lights

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:14.670
and a parent became aware that
because I like the teacher,

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:16.920
she was very nice, the
principal was nice, [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:18.780
functioned well, and
I dropped my kid off,

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:19.780
and they seemed to be learning.

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:20.850
They were bringing
back work packets.

00:24:20.850 --> 00:24:22.725
And then all of a sudden
we flip on the light

00:24:22.725 --> 00:24:26.700
and say that's an F school,
immediately change happened.

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:30.030
And people became
frustrated and angry.

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:32.640
And so that's really changed
Florida more than anything.

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:34.720
Second was just this
advancement of Choice.

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:37.410
So we have about 3
million school children.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:41.580
If we can ever get to
1.5 across that Rubicon--

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:44.640
we're probably there now with
about half a million children

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:46.740
in Choice, because we can't--

00:24:46.740 --> 00:24:49.920
even if you had a Gwen Graham
or Andrew Gillum or whoever come

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:51.870
in-- a Nancy Pelosi in Florida--

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:53.670
you can't take
those 500,000 kids

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:56.380
and bring them back into
the public school system.

00:24:56.380 --> 00:24:58.650
So you have to keep
doing what we're doing

00:24:58.650 --> 00:24:59.910
as quickly as we're doing it.

00:24:59.910 --> 00:25:01.410
Dr. Arnn was talking
about Tennessee

00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:05.053
asking for 100 Barney
Charter Schools.

00:25:05.053 --> 00:25:05.970
That's a game changer.

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:07.890
Once you have that,
and all of a sudden

00:25:07.890 --> 00:25:09.450
the governor leaves
who's fantastic--

00:25:09.450 --> 00:25:12.630
Governor Lee's gone and it's a
liberal that comes in there--

00:25:12.630 --> 00:25:17.110
you can't put the
animals back in the barn.

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:18.960
So yeah, it's
happening, but it only

00:25:18.960 --> 00:25:20.460
happens when the
leadership is good.

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:22.540
Where the leadership
is bad, I mean,

00:25:22.540 --> 00:25:24.990
all you have to do is read
the articles under a pandemic.

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:25.532
I don't care.

00:25:25.532 --> 00:25:30.268
Take the top 10, five
largest urban districts--

00:25:30.268 --> 00:25:32.310
school districts in the
United States of America.

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:33.810
Florida has five.

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:37.020
All five of ours were open
with face-to-face instruction

00:25:37.020 --> 00:25:38.520
for all children.

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:40.170
The other five were closed--

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:42.935
most of them until
January or February.

00:25:42.935 --> 00:25:44.310
And some are still
closed today--

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:45.810
Boston, Chicago, Los Angeles.

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:48.348
It's still-- you're never
getting those kids back.

00:25:48.348 --> 00:25:49.890
You're never getting
those kids back.

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:51.270
And so but why?

00:25:51.270 --> 00:25:54.240
I mean, it's the difference
between Gavin Newsom and Ron

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:55.890
DeSantis.

00:25:55.890 --> 00:25:57.720
You know, Cuomo
was writing books

00:25:57.720 --> 00:26:00.120
about how to survive
in a pandemic

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:03.180
while arguably 10,000
senior citizens died because

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:05.400
of his failures, while
Ron DeSantis was studying

00:26:05.400 --> 00:26:07.770
the science and the metrics
and the first thing he did

00:26:07.770 --> 00:26:10.020
was protect the nursing homes.

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:11.430
Leadership's everything.

00:26:11.430 --> 00:26:15.470
So yes, under good leadership
we're making great strides.

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:18.130
SPEAKER: Question in the back.

00:26:18.130 --> 00:26:20.390
AUDIENCE: Thank you, sir.

00:26:20.390 --> 00:26:23.600
One thing I've
wondered-- it seems

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:26.630
like a very ideological
debate that we're

00:26:26.630 --> 00:26:29.570
having, and maybe
not always connected

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:32.660
to outcomes in a beneficial way.

00:26:32.660 --> 00:26:37.070
And it appears that
quite frequently it's

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:40.380
hard to find common ground.

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:45.030
What have you found to be a
good argument or a good way

00:26:45.030 --> 00:26:47.880
to approach this discussion
that helps you find

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:51.960
common ground with people who
may have an adverse reaction

00:26:51.960 --> 00:26:53.520
to your ideological position?

00:26:56.980 --> 00:26:58.900
RICHARD CORCORAN:
I'm trying not to--

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:00.910
I grew up in an Irish
Catholic family with five,

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:03.370
so the dinner table was
always like arguments.

00:27:03.370 --> 00:27:07.288
And the way I found common
ground is literally-- and Dr.

00:27:07.288 --> 00:27:08.830
Arnn, again, was
talking about this--

00:27:08.830 --> 00:27:09.918
I have fought.

00:27:09.918 --> 00:27:11.710
The way I find common
ground-- because they

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:12.760
will roll over you.

00:27:12.760 --> 00:27:16.360
When we wanted to open
up schools in September,

00:27:16.360 --> 00:27:19.420
the entire agency
was surrounded.

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:21.400
We know we have a
small courtyard.

00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:26.590
The entire agency was surrounded
by literally fake coffins,

00:27:26.590 --> 00:27:28.390
headstones--

00:27:28.390 --> 00:27:31.720
the grim reaper was walking
around the building.

00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:37.540
And they sued me to
keep schools closed.

00:27:37.540 --> 00:27:40.090
I think, I always
say I'm always open.

00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:42.550
I'll sit down with anybody
and have that discussion.

00:27:42.550 --> 00:27:46.180
I do think who I'm
describing, though--

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:49.060
I think if you're
talking about people--

00:27:49.060 --> 00:27:53.770
can a Joe Biden sit down
and have a conversation

00:27:53.770 --> 00:27:56.170
with a Tom Cotton?

00:27:56.170 --> 00:27:57.760
Yes, I think they can.

00:27:57.760 --> 00:28:01.390
But I'm talking on that
outer side, and really

00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:03.520
its outer left.

00:28:03.520 --> 00:28:04.630
There's no negotiation.

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:06.588
I don't think Antifa
wants to sit down and have

00:28:06.588 --> 00:28:10.380
a conversation with me about how
can we make the society better.

00:28:10.380 --> 00:28:12.550
And again, Dr. Arnn
gave a great analogy

00:28:12.550 --> 00:28:15.310
about the warring in
the streets in Germany

00:28:15.310 --> 00:28:18.285
pre-World War II was between
the Nazis and the Germans,

00:28:18.285 --> 00:28:18.910
the communists.

00:28:18.910 --> 00:28:22.240
And the Nazis ultimately won,
and Hitler became the leader

00:28:22.240 --> 00:28:24.115
of the Nazi street fighters.

00:28:24.115 --> 00:28:25.990
That's what I think
you're seeing with Antifa

00:28:25.990 --> 00:28:26.740
and what have you.

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:31.490
What they want is
complete upheaval.

00:28:31.490 --> 00:28:34.270
And so those people
I just don't--

00:28:34.270 --> 00:28:36.590
where necessary
I'll sit with them,

00:28:36.590 --> 00:28:38.170
but I think we have
to be efficient

00:28:38.170 --> 00:28:42.100
and very strategic in how we
go about what we're doing.

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:46.270
And the war will be
won in education.

00:28:46.270 --> 00:28:47.800
If we can get
education right, we

00:28:47.800 --> 00:28:51.880
can have kids be literate
and then understand

00:28:51.880 --> 00:28:55.150
what it means to be a
self-governing citizen

00:28:55.150 --> 00:28:58.720
in a self-governing
country, we'll win it back.

00:28:58.720 --> 00:28:59.950
I think Dr. Arnn, again--

00:28:59.950 --> 00:29:01.950
I keep going-- we just
spent some time with him.

00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:05.140
But he said that
education is our sword.

00:29:05.140 --> 00:29:07.120
That's our weapon.

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:08.482
Our weapon is education.

00:29:08.482 --> 00:29:09.190
And we can do it.

00:29:09.190 --> 00:29:10.970
We can get it right.

00:29:10.970 --> 00:29:12.940
And I do think--

00:29:12.940 --> 00:29:15.190
I always try not
to sound biased,

00:29:15.190 --> 00:29:17.178
but I do think that we
are willing to sit down.

00:29:17.178 --> 00:29:18.970
We are willing to have
those conversations,

00:29:18.970 --> 00:29:21.100
and we are willing to
look at the science.

00:29:21.100 --> 00:29:24.650
All the far left yelling at
us about science, science,

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:26.650
science, but when it came
to opening up schools,

00:29:26.650 --> 00:29:30.340
every single thing that has been
said in January and February

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:32.290
post the Biden
administration taking over

00:29:32.290 --> 00:29:34.840
and the CDC and all the
changes, and now it's OK.

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:35.620
It's safe to open.

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:36.550
Kids don't transmit.

00:29:36.550 --> 00:29:38.440
Kids aren't in harm's way.

00:29:38.440 --> 00:29:41.170
All of that science was
100% available to us

00:29:41.170 --> 00:29:45.380
in June and July, which is
what we made our decision on.

00:29:45.380 --> 00:29:47.588
So I think we're willing to
have those conversations.

00:29:47.588 --> 00:29:48.797
We're willing to be rational.

00:29:48.797 --> 00:29:50.980
We're willing to look at
the science and the facts.

00:29:50.980 --> 00:29:52.150
I mean, the truth is the truth.

00:29:52.150 --> 00:29:53.317
This room believes in truth.

00:29:53.317 --> 00:29:55.180
We believe it's objective.

00:29:55.180 --> 00:29:59.778
I had lunch yesterday with
Janet and David Wayland,

00:29:59.778 --> 00:30:02.320
and they were talking about how
they were educated in Kansas.

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:03.880
And I just thought
it was a great line.

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:05.630
But her professor used
to say all the time

00:30:05.630 --> 00:30:09.750
to the doubters in the class as
they're going through learning

00:30:09.750 --> 00:30:10.600
the great books.

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:12.940
And he would say, here's
what I'll tell you.

00:30:12.940 --> 00:30:16.840
Fire is hot, water is
wet, and rocks are hard.

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:19.180
I mean, it's just
a beautiful way

00:30:19.180 --> 00:30:21.280
of saying the
truth is the truth.

00:30:21.280 --> 00:30:23.320
But they want to deny that.

00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:25.990
I mean, the whole argument
on university campuses--

00:30:25.990 --> 00:30:26.830
there is no truth.

00:30:26.830 --> 00:30:28.000
It's all subjective.

00:30:28.000 --> 00:30:32.300
And so there's going
to be a battle.

00:30:32.300 --> 00:30:34.720
And so sit down,
have that discussion,

00:30:34.720 --> 00:30:36.427
be transparent, be open always.

00:30:36.427 --> 00:30:37.760
I think all of us would do that.

00:30:37.760 --> 00:30:40.177
But the way we're going to get
to where we're going to get

00:30:40.177 --> 00:30:42.962
is by fighting every
step of the way.

00:30:42.962 --> 00:30:44.920
It will be a battle, and
we will have to fight.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:48.010
And as Winston Churchill
says, all the other virtues

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:50.230
are great, but courage
is the most important,

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:53.190
because without it, none
of the others are possible.

00:30:53.190 --> 00:30:55.738
SPEAKER: Question to
the speaker's left.

00:30:55.738 --> 00:30:57.780
LANA THEIS: Hi, State
Senator Lana Theis up here.

00:30:57.780 --> 00:30:57.920
Hi.

00:30:57.920 --> 00:30:58.390
RICHARD CORCORAN: Hi.

00:30:58.390 --> 00:30:59.932
LANA THEIS: Thank
you for being here.

00:30:59.932 --> 00:31:01.890
So grateful.

00:31:01.890 --> 00:31:05.760
Michigan has lost 65,000 kids
from our public education

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:08.220
system, largely because
of the shutdown.

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:10.770
I'm wondering-- excuse me--

00:31:10.770 --> 00:31:14.070
if Florida experienced any kind
of a loss, and what that looked

00:31:14.070 --> 00:31:15.060
like.

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:19.230
Since you stayed open, I would
assume probably not, but more

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:21.877
than the normal loss.

00:31:21.877 --> 00:31:23.210
RICHARD CORCORAN: We track that.

00:31:26.340 --> 00:31:28.590
And I'm just talking
students, because we have,

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:31.162
as Michigan does, how
we do our funding form,

00:31:31.162 --> 00:31:32.620
there's weights
for certain things.

00:31:32.620 --> 00:31:36.120
So one student could actually
count for two students,

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:38.110
just based on the
weights and funding.

00:31:38.110 --> 00:31:39.900
But if you look at
actual students,

00:31:39.900 --> 00:31:42.750
there was maybe a 5,000
or 10,000 difference.

00:31:42.750 --> 00:31:45.150
And a lot of them, when
we tracked them down--

00:31:45.150 --> 00:31:46.860
because we were awarded the
districts for tracking them

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:47.430
down--

00:31:47.430 --> 00:31:50.880
most of them were parents who
went to the homeschooling mode

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:53.670
without properly
withdrawing their child.

00:31:53.670 --> 00:31:57.330
So it comes up as an extra
10,000 lost students,

00:31:57.330 --> 00:32:00.360
but we found that most of them
were home school students.

00:32:00.360 --> 00:32:04.200
But every year we lose
10,000 to 15,000 students.

00:32:04.200 --> 00:32:06.150
We are a growth state.

00:32:06.150 --> 00:32:08.520
1,000 people come pretty
much every single day.

00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:10.410
But we also have a lot
of families leaving,

00:32:10.410 --> 00:32:12.030
especially when we have
a Hurricane Michael come

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:13.050
through the panhandle.

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:15.050
You'll see a lot of the
low income families will

00:32:15.050 --> 00:32:17.490
go to Alabama or Mississippi.

00:32:17.490 --> 00:32:19.650
And so we always
have that, but we

00:32:19.650 --> 00:32:21.565
do a good job of tracking them.

00:32:21.565 --> 00:32:23.190
And because we were
open, it was easier

00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:26.730
to do so, because
basically if 95 kids came

00:32:26.730 --> 00:32:31.268
back to a class or a school
we knew that was supposed

00:32:31.268 --> 00:32:33.060
to be 100, we could
immediately know here's

00:32:33.060 --> 00:32:34.230
the five we've got to find.

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:36.570
It makes it a lot easier
when you're tracking them.

00:32:36.570 --> 00:32:38.670
As opposed to-- I mean,
look at Los Angeles.

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:41.970
They have online, synchronous
learning, asynchronous--

00:32:41.970 --> 00:32:43.320
they've tried everything.

00:32:43.320 --> 00:32:45.990
And I think there's some days
where only like 15% or 20%

00:32:45.990 --> 00:32:48.720
of the students are
actually clicking in online.

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:50.430
I mean, it's just a disaster.

00:32:50.430 --> 00:32:53.520
I've said to people,
it's not sad.

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:55.650
It's not frustrating.

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:57.990
What's going on in the rest
of the country is criminal.

00:32:57.990 --> 00:33:01.590
If I was to go to any one of
your homes, and grab your kids,

00:33:01.590 --> 00:33:04.068
and put them in solitude,
and say they can't hang out

00:33:04.068 --> 00:33:05.610
and they have to do
all of the stuff,

00:33:05.610 --> 00:33:09.293
I'd be arrested for a
host of different crimes.

00:33:09.293 --> 00:33:11.460
And then if I said to their
parents, and by the way,

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:13.560
you're going to have to close
down your small business.

00:33:13.560 --> 00:33:15.450
You can't work anymore
because you're going have

00:33:15.450 --> 00:33:16.320
to take care of your kids--

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:17.700
I'd be arrested for
a host of crimes.

00:33:17.700 --> 00:33:19.450
What's going on in the
rest of the country

00:33:19.450 --> 00:33:23.377
is literally criminal taking
and criminal behavior.

00:33:23.377 --> 00:33:25.460
SPEAKER: There's a question
to the speaker's left.

00:33:25.460 --> 00:33:29.018
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:29.018 --> 00:33:29.560
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:31.870
My question regards
the curriculum

00:33:31.870 --> 00:33:35.020
and what role the
state has, if any,

00:33:35.020 --> 00:33:38.020
in oversight of local
school curriculums.

00:33:38.020 --> 00:33:40.990
And I have a little story
that I'd like to insert here.

00:33:40.990 --> 00:33:44.368
Two Thanksgivings ago, we
had a family celebration,

00:33:44.368 --> 00:33:45.910
and I found out that
there were going

00:33:45.910 --> 00:33:48.922
to be two children--
one 8, one 10 attending.

00:33:48.922 --> 00:33:51.130
So my husband and I thought
it would be really cute--

00:33:51.130 --> 00:33:52.780
I have these costumes--

00:33:52.780 --> 00:33:53.908
to dress up as pilgrims.

00:33:53.908 --> 00:33:56.200
We thought that the kids--
when we opened the door they

00:33:56.200 --> 00:33:58.000
saw two pilgrims, and
it was Thanksgiving--

00:33:58.000 --> 00:33:59.980
that they'd get a
kick out of it, right?

00:33:59.980 --> 00:34:02.990
So we kept the costumes
on for the entire day.

00:34:02.990 --> 00:34:05.830
So we're sitting around the
table in our pilgrim costumes.

00:34:05.830 --> 00:34:06.790
My husband had a hat.

00:34:06.790 --> 00:34:08.440
I had my little--

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:13.612
And finally, the 10-year-old
at one point looked at us and--

00:34:13.612 --> 00:34:15.820
because I'm starting to give
the story of the Indians

00:34:15.820 --> 00:34:18.159
and the Pilgrims and
the meal and the Indians

00:34:18.159 --> 00:34:20.050
really helped out the
pilgrims because it

00:34:20.050 --> 00:34:20.980
was the first winter.

00:34:20.980 --> 00:34:23.770
I give the whole spiel
that we grew up with.

00:34:23.770 --> 00:34:27.610
And this 10-year-old
looks at me, and he says,

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:32.400
didn't the pilgrims come to
kill the Indigenous people?

00:34:32.400 --> 00:34:36.030
I mean, talk about putting
a damper on things.

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:38.219
So there we were
dressed as pilgrims,

00:34:38.219 --> 00:34:40.500
and in the eyes of
this little boy,

00:34:40.500 --> 00:34:42.570
he had learned that
the pilgrims came

00:34:42.570 --> 00:34:44.670
to kill the Indigenous Indians.

00:34:44.670 --> 00:34:47.070
So we tried to set
things straight,

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:48.690
but then we changed the topic.

00:34:48.690 --> 00:34:50.280
And the mother
went, well you know,

00:34:50.280 --> 00:34:53.250
they do go to a
progressive school.

00:34:53.250 --> 00:34:56.370
And I said, well,
I was just shocked

00:34:56.370 --> 00:34:59.220
to find out-- to even
hear the word Indigenous

00:34:59.220 --> 00:35:01.410
in the mouth of a 10-year-old.

00:35:01.410 --> 00:35:03.900
You know, why would he say
they came to kill the Indians?

00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:06.067
I said, it sounds to me
like your progressive school

00:35:06.067 --> 00:35:08.327
is indoctrinating.

00:35:08.327 --> 00:35:10.660
And that was the end, and
then we just kind of moved on.

00:35:10.660 --> 00:35:13.230
So then I found out
about the 1619 Project

00:35:13.230 --> 00:35:15.430
that I had never
heard of before.

00:35:15.430 --> 00:35:19.440
So all these years, our children
have been reading books--

00:35:19.440 --> 00:35:20.730
being taught books--

00:35:20.730 --> 00:35:23.070
that present things
a certain way.

00:35:23.070 --> 00:35:26.430
And I bet you that a
good majority of parents

00:35:26.430 --> 00:35:28.350
are totally unaware.

00:35:28.350 --> 00:35:31.787
Oh, and by the way, they didn't
know what the Mayflower was.

00:35:31.787 --> 00:35:33.620
Because I got into the
boat-- they came over

00:35:33.620 --> 00:35:35.540
on this boat, the Mayflower.

00:35:35.540 --> 00:35:38.420
No, we haven't heard
about the Mayflower.

00:35:38.420 --> 00:35:41.960
OK, so how are you addressing--
and that's just one example.

00:35:41.960 --> 00:35:45.920
Critical race theory
is another one that's

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:48.110
permeated our curriculum.

00:35:48.110 --> 00:35:53.520
Who decides what books are
going to be used in instruction?

00:35:53.520 --> 00:35:55.270
RICHARD CORCORAN:
That's a great question.

00:35:55.270 --> 00:35:56.645
So we've just
fought that battle,

00:35:56.645 --> 00:35:58.320
and we still fight
it on a daily basis.

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:00.270
And there's multiple
ways you can fight it.

00:36:00.270 --> 00:36:02.760
So first, we rewrote
all our standards.

00:36:02.760 --> 00:36:06.195
So we're on a standard
five-year book adoption,

00:36:06.195 --> 00:36:08.070
or seven-year book
adoption, depending if you

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:10.920
add in certain science years.

00:36:10.920 --> 00:36:12.900
And so we disrupted
the whole book adoption

00:36:12.900 --> 00:36:15.270
because we rewrote
our whole standards.

00:36:15.270 --> 00:36:17.910
And we rewrote them with
the science of reading.

00:36:17.910 --> 00:36:20.160
We have the first
in the nation--

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:23.010
a civics reading list
from kindergarten

00:36:23.010 --> 00:36:24.970
all the way through 12th grade.

00:36:24.970 --> 00:36:28.200
We have a reading list
for all other courses.

00:36:28.200 --> 00:36:32.937
It's just content rich,
and the quick anecdote,

00:36:32.937 --> 00:36:34.020
which you guys know well--

00:36:34.020 --> 00:36:36.150
I probably stole it from
you guys when you came.

00:36:36.150 --> 00:36:38.565
But they have basically
kids who are 1s and 2s who

00:36:38.565 --> 00:36:40.290
are functionally or
somewhat illiterate,

00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:41.490
and they have the
3s, 4s, and 5s.

00:36:41.490 --> 00:36:43.448
And they put them in a
room, and they give them

00:36:43.448 --> 00:36:46.830
a reading comprehension
exam, and it's on baseball.

00:36:46.830 --> 00:36:48.620
All of the 1s and
2s play baseball.

00:36:48.620 --> 00:36:50.383
None of the 3s, 4s,
and 5s play baseball.

00:36:50.383 --> 00:36:52.050
And then they grade
and see how they did

00:36:52.050 --> 00:36:53.340
on the reading comprehension.

00:36:53.340 --> 00:36:56.880
And the 1s and 2s scored 4-0,
40% higher than the 3s, 4s,

00:36:56.880 --> 00:36:59.280
and 5s, even though they
couldn't read nearly

00:36:59.280 --> 00:37:01.072
as fast or as well or at all.

00:37:01.072 --> 00:37:02.280
But they understood the game.

00:37:02.280 --> 00:37:04.560
And so obviously
content matters.

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:07.530
The vocabulary matters
as much as the decoding.

00:37:07.530 --> 00:37:09.420
And so we rewrote
all of our standards.

00:37:09.420 --> 00:37:10.650
We did all of that stuff.

00:37:10.650 --> 00:37:13.190
And then we do a
book adoption, which

00:37:13.190 --> 00:37:14.940
now all the publishers,
and the publishers

00:37:14.940 --> 00:37:16.680
are just infested with liberals.

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:18.953
And so we would have to say
to them in our bid specs,

00:37:18.953 --> 00:37:20.370
we're not going
to take or approve

00:37:20.370 --> 00:37:22.770
your bid unless you have--

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:24.990
a certain percentage
of our reading list

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:27.120
has to be in your text.

00:37:27.120 --> 00:37:30.762
You can have no whole
language or blended literacy

00:37:30.762 --> 00:37:32.970
or anything, and it has to
be the science of reading.

00:37:32.970 --> 00:37:34.620
And we went through
a whole criteria.

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:37.950
And then when the books came
in, we evaluate the books

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:39.720
on the criteria we said.

00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:43.740
But I was just saying last night
to the folks that we had dinner

00:37:43.740 --> 00:37:46.350
with, so now the
books have come back.

00:37:46.350 --> 00:37:48.507
And you don't think--

00:37:48.507 --> 00:37:51.090
and, of course, COVID hit, so
we were a little bit distracted.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:52.715
But I didn't think
to say, OK, keep all

00:37:52.715 --> 00:37:54.270
of the crazy liberal stuff out.

00:37:54.270 --> 00:37:56.190
And so now we
literally have them,

00:37:56.190 --> 00:37:59.550
and they hide it in
social emotional learning.

00:37:59.550 --> 00:38:01.988
So it doesn't say
critical race theory,

00:38:01.988 --> 00:38:03.780
but you could definitely
have a teacher who

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:04.930
teaches critical race theory.

00:38:04.930 --> 00:38:06.210
So now we have to go
back to them and say,

00:38:06.210 --> 00:38:07.777
if it's electronic,
we want it out.

00:38:07.777 --> 00:38:09.360
And then on top of
that, we're passing

00:38:09.360 --> 00:38:13.670
a rule this coming month that
says for the 185,000 teachers,

00:38:13.670 --> 00:38:15.420
you can't indoctrinate
students with stuff

00:38:15.420 --> 00:38:17.940
that's not based
on our standards--

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:19.050
the new best standards.

00:38:19.050 --> 00:38:22.300
So all of that, but you have to
police them on a daily basis.

00:38:22.300 --> 00:38:25.140
It's 185,000 teachers in
a classroom with anywhere

00:38:25.140 --> 00:38:28.110
from 18 to 25 kids, and if
you're not physically there

00:38:28.110 --> 00:38:29.160
in the classroom--

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:31.290
I will tell you, it's
working in the universities,

00:38:31.290 --> 00:38:32.880
and it's starting to work in--

00:38:32.880 --> 00:38:36.270
I've censored or fired or
terminated numerous teachers

00:38:36.270 --> 00:38:37.078
for doing that.

00:38:37.078 --> 00:38:39.120
I'm getting sued right
now in Duval County, which

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:40.770
is Jacksonville,
because there was

00:38:40.770 --> 00:38:44.650
an entire classroom memorialized
to Black Lives Matter.

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:46.920
We made sure she was
terminated, and now we're

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:49.800
being sued by every one
of the liberal left groups

00:38:49.800 --> 00:38:52.200
for freedom of speech issues.

00:38:52.200 --> 00:38:55.980
And I say to them-- to the
point of your question--

00:38:55.980 --> 00:38:57.450
try to find common ground.

00:38:57.450 --> 00:38:59.160
I'll say, look, let's
not even talk about

00:38:59.160 --> 00:39:02.280
whether it's right
or true or good--

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:03.150
what you have there.

00:39:03.150 --> 00:39:06.090
My issue is when you're
a third grade teacher

00:39:06.090 --> 00:39:09.813
and only 42%, 41% of your
kids are on grade level,

00:39:09.813 --> 00:39:10.980
why don't you do me a favor?

00:39:10.980 --> 00:39:13.480
Get them on grade level and
then we'll have that discussion.

00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:16.170
Make them at least to be able to
read and understand it first--

00:39:16.170 --> 00:39:19.080
to try to not act
like I'm radical right

00:39:19.080 --> 00:39:20.760
or whatever they
want to call me,

00:39:20.760 --> 00:39:23.070
but it's a constant
vigilant fight.

00:39:23.070 --> 00:39:25.750
It's a terrible battle.

00:39:25.750 --> 00:39:28.382
SPEAKER: We now have time
for one last question.

00:39:28.382 --> 00:39:30.590
AUDIENCE: Thank you for
coming all the way from sunny

00:39:30.590 --> 00:39:32.570
and free Florida
to join us here.

00:39:32.570 --> 00:39:36.590
RICHARD CORCORAN: Yesterday,
you rivaled Florida.

00:39:36.590 --> 00:39:38.660
AUDIENCE: The weather
followed you here.

00:39:38.660 --> 00:39:41.480
Dr. Arnn often talks
about education

00:39:41.480 --> 00:39:46.260
being a partnership between
the educators and the parents.

00:39:46.260 --> 00:39:49.520
We've had this huge
influx of federal cash.

00:39:49.520 --> 00:39:53.150
What are some steps that Florida
is taking to help partner

00:39:53.150 --> 00:39:55.520
with parents and
empower parents,

00:39:55.520 --> 00:39:57.860
specifically with some of
the federal money that's

00:39:57.860 --> 00:39:58.510
been coming in?

00:39:58.510 --> 00:40:01.010
RICHARD CORCORAN: We've taken
most of the federal money that

00:40:01.010 --> 00:40:01.610
wasn't--

00:40:01.610 --> 00:40:05.743
they've gotten more prescriptive
in the last ARP plan.

00:40:05.743 --> 00:40:07.160
But before that,
we basically have

00:40:07.160 --> 00:40:11.630
been using it to
invest on that pre-K

00:40:11.630 --> 00:40:14.870
all the way to third grade in
massive ways involving parents.

00:40:14.870 --> 00:40:20.630
And then on top of that, trying
to grab hold of the learning

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:23.930
gain losses that we have
and the learning gap.

00:40:23.930 --> 00:40:25.250
I mean, in Florida we have--

00:40:25.250 --> 00:40:27.590
so I asked my staff--

00:40:27.590 --> 00:40:31.100
right now on the third grade
FSA, we're at about 58%

00:40:31.100 --> 00:40:34.730
3s, 4s, and 5s, 42% below--

00:40:34.730 --> 00:40:37.258
partially or fully illiterate.

00:40:37.258 --> 00:40:39.800
Which I say that, because one
or two just doesn't-- it sounds

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:40.317
innocuous.

00:40:40.317 --> 00:40:42.650
I want you to understand these
kids are never coming out

00:40:42.650 --> 00:40:44.900
of that for the most part.

00:40:44.900 --> 00:40:48.350
And then I said, OK, if I wanted
to at the current growth rate--

00:40:48.350 --> 00:40:50.367
we are absolutely--
if you go to any

00:40:50.367 --> 00:40:51.950
of these national
conferences, they'll

00:40:51.950 --> 00:40:55.580
tell you Florida leads the
country in education reform.

00:40:55.580 --> 00:40:57.860
And what we're doing,
most other states copy.

00:40:57.860 --> 00:41:01.220
And most stats-- we have the
number one AP test takers.

00:41:01.220 --> 00:41:03.047
We have the number
two performance.

00:41:03.047 --> 00:41:04.130
And this is all pre-COVID.

00:41:04.130 --> 00:41:06.088
I think you're going to
see us go to number one

00:41:06.088 --> 00:41:08.150
in most categories
because of our kids

00:41:08.150 --> 00:41:09.440
having that instruction.

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:13.550
But besides that-- so Florida
doing well in reform--

00:41:13.550 --> 00:41:16.670
what would it take at
our current growth rate,

00:41:16.670 --> 00:41:20.780
because we started at way below.

00:41:20.780 --> 00:41:21.470
I used to joke.

00:41:21.470 --> 00:41:22.230
It's a terrible thing.

00:41:22.230 --> 00:41:24.105
I hope I don't offend
anyone for Mississippi.

00:41:24.105 --> 00:41:26.292
But I used to joke, thank
God for back in 1998--

00:41:26.292 --> 00:41:28.250
thank God there was
Mississippi, otherwise we'd

00:41:28.250 --> 00:41:29.660
be dead last in every category.

00:41:29.660 --> 00:41:31.910
But Mississippi was, I think,
dead last in everything.

00:41:31.910 --> 00:41:36.170
But what would it do to
close the achievement gap?

00:41:36.170 --> 00:41:40.430
And what would it take to get
non-Title I or non-low income

00:41:40.430 --> 00:41:43.640
kids to 90% proficiency
in third grade?

00:41:43.640 --> 00:41:46.610
And what would it take for
Title I or low income kids

00:41:46.610 --> 00:41:50.600
to get to 90% proficiency
by third grade?

00:41:50.600 --> 00:41:53.720
And the staggering number
is to take basically

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:56.780
two parent, middle
class and up kids--

00:41:56.780 --> 00:41:59.210
to get them from
their current state

00:41:59.210 --> 00:42:02.240
to 90% proficiency in
Florida would take 100 years.

00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:05.180
It would take 200 years to
get low income kids there.

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:06.800
And the overwhelming problem--

00:42:06.800 --> 00:42:09.320
I mean, there is an
organized injustice

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:15.560
that's going on in education,
with basically our problem is

00:42:15.560 --> 00:42:16.400
low income kids.

00:42:16.400 --> 00:42:18.560
And with all the
Barney schools, you're

00:42:18.560 --> 00:42:21.920
starting to see the classical
schools get more and more

00:42:21.920 --> 00:42:23.960
of these low income kids--

00:42:23.960 --> 00:42:26.810
to your question, sir, that
now it's going to really help.

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:30.440
I mean, we're going into
the nontraditional sector

00:42:30.440 --> 00:42:31.740
of these students.

00:42:31.740 --> 00:42:35.010
And so what you're
seeing, though,

00:42:35.010 --> 00:42:42.660
with that huge chasm is unless
you have just complete Clayton

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:45.175
Christensen disruption,
you're going

00:42:45.175 --> 00:42:46.800
to have this organized
injustice, which

00:42:46.800 --> 00:42:47.700
really is this--

00:42:47.700 --> 00:42:52.440
I say it not publicly, but
the bulk of those low income

00:42:52.440 --> 00:42:56.298
kids are majority minority.

00:42:56.298 --> 00:42:57.840
And these are our
struggling readers.

00:42:57.840 --> 00:43:00.870
Our struggling readers are
overwhelming majority poor.

00:43:00.870 --> 00:43:03.750
Of that poor population,
overwhelming majority minority.

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:06.420
Of that population,
overwhelmingly majority

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:07.410
African-American.

00:43:07.410 --> 00:43:10.800
So there is an injustice
that's going on.

00:43:10.800 --> 00:43:14.190
Instead of having marches
and riots and what have you,

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:17.130
there's something that could be
done, but it's not being done.

00:43:17.130 --> 00:43:20.432
But the more we
empower those parents--

00:43:20.432 --> 00:43:22.140
And what we found in
the Choice movement,

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:23.557
people would
literally say to us--

00:43:23.557 --> 00:43:25.800
when we started the
Transformation on Choice back

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:28.730
in 1998, they'd be, you can't.

00:43:28.730 --> 00:43:32.220
Literally, these would be things
that the left would say to us.

00:43:32.220 --> 00:43:33.040
You can't do that.

00:43:33.040 --> 00:43:35.310
You can't just
turn over a voucher

00:43:35.310 --> 00:43:37.530
and give it to a single
mom who doesn't even

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:38.655
have a job or an education.

00:43:38.655 --> 00:43:39.948
It would be a disaster.

00:43:39.948 --> 00:43:41.490
They would literally
say that to us--

00:43:41.490 --> 00:43:44.670
I mean, a patently
racist statement.

00:43:44.670 --> 00:43:46.530
And yet when we did
that, what we found

00:43:46.530 --> 00:43:47.940
is guess what every parent does?

00:43:47.940 --> 00:43:50.523
Every parent cares about their
kids far more than a bureaucrat

00:43:50.523 --> 00:43:53.460
sitting in some administration
building in city hall.

00:43:53.460 --> 00:43:55.933
And they do care, and they
do move with their feet

00:43:55.933 --> 00:43:58.350
to the point that the charter
school movement in Florida--

00:43:58.350 --> 00:44:00.420
I can't say this for the
rest of the country--

00:44:00.420 --> 00:44:02.700
we have more minority students
in our public charter schools

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:04.283
than we do in our
traditional schools.

00:44:04.283 --> 00:44:07.050
And we have the same
and sometimes higher

00:44:07.050 --> 00:44:09.255
low income kids in
our charter schools

00:44:09.255 --> 00:44:11.130
than we do in our
traditional public schools.

00:44:11.130 --> 00:44:12.630
And all are public.

00:44:12.630 --> 00:44:14.047
Because parents
will get involved,

00:44:14.047 --> 00:44:15.380
and they will make a difference.

00:44:15.380 --> 00:44:17.250
And the results are
anywhere from 10 to 15--

00:44:17.250 --> 00:44:20.340
on a bad day-- better than in
the traditional public schools.

00:44:20.340 --> 00:44:23.520
So we know that the more
you involve those parents,

00:44:23.520 --> 00:44:26.350
you will have better
outcomes for the students.

00:44:26.350 --> 00:44:29.020
AUDIENCE: Please join me in
thanking Commissioner Corcoran.

